ght  on our production stack we still had to  go with the old stuff  so we ended up with a push into  going into a new production stack and  this all was still before we introduced  node.js so we had bootstrap in the front  end  we had javascript templating as i said  and  then if you want to go for our regular  stacks  i said that we have c plus and java so  what we did is we  injected the c plus v8 engine into our c  plus deck that was fairly easy we call  that the project delorean  so if you remember delorean from the  back to the future  it's that awesome car that does the time  jumping stuff so we thought it was kind  of  a fit and then there is mozilla's rhino  which is a full javascript  implementation in java  so that's what we used on our java stack  to run the different templates  and looking at that basically we had now  a few different kind of environments  again prototyping  we went with dust and it was great now  since we settled on using  dust also on our java stack we leveraged  rhino  and we were able to run the same  templates that we wrote for our  prototyping stack  on production as well and that was  obviously great for us because it helped  us to  not leave the old stacks behind because  if you want to push for  new apps it's obviously an easy choice  to say we want to go for nodes and we  want to go for  awesome javascript templates and we  leave the old stuff behind but since we  have so many different applications  that was not really something that we  could do  so we wanted to try out notes in  production  and this one thought this one wish  came from a simple project paypal  checkout page that you might know if you  go  and buy something online and it  redirects you to paypal  generates more than 3.5 billion dollars  every year  and we thought why not start at  the core of paypal at the one thing that  drives most of our revenue  so a project got named after we got  hermes  gots born and hermes is the god of  agility and mobility  so what we did is we started with a very  simple stack that we just introduced  at the beginning of that week so we had  node.js running we used bootstrap to do  some  very lean ui and we used some other open  source as i mentioned we used  bower and the first prototype of the new  checkout platform was born in three days  and people were incredibly excited  because we didn't have to  fiddle around with our old frameworks it  was much easier for  new engineers to get involved so that  was great  and then we thought we should definitely  try this with another project  and that's the account page the paypal  wallet app  so if you go to paypal and log in over  there and you see your balance and your  funding sources and all that stuff  that is actually the first project that  we did  and it's actually kind of risk to go for  a new platform in production if you're  not sure that it's really running  so what we did is we had first this  initial project if we checkout page and  people were convinced we should try it  right  so we had two teams starting with two  projects the first team was a  traditional java team that was used to  our stack  and was well trained in actually  building apps on this deck  the second team was only two javascript  engineers compared to five java  engineers  and they were supposed to just give it a  try and build the same kind of  experience  so we started in january in 2013  and the java team obviously used the  existing components and started building  the app  in march just two months after the  environment for node was completely set  up so we had  key stores we had logging services we uh  we knew how to connect to our databases  and so on so that's what we two  engineers built on javascript  now in june the two teams met crossroads  so in terms of functionality both  applications were on power  one team five java engineers started  with all the existing services  the other team was just two node  engineers  so we ran a few functionality tests and  all of them passed and we were actually  really convinced that this is a good  sign  and run a few benchmarks so  i don't want to bore you too much with  numbers but just to quickly go into it  we have a bunch of different requests  with number of users  and this is the javastack and you see  the java stack actually averages  at some point at about 11 pages per  seconds  starting with one user per request  and then we go higher up to 15 users  that request at the same time  now this is five cores for actually  production hardware that we are using  and then we had the note stack and the  node stack  actually serves nearly wv requests and  the same stuff happens just on one core  at that moment  so it was a very young new stack  it was barely optimized and was more  efficient straight away  obviously you cannot really compare  apples to peaches  so it's not completely fair to compare  those two  because one is using our proprietary  java kind of stack  the other not but at least for us it was  a good sign that we could go ahead with  this  a few more numbers that we had were  pretty much the codes was actually very  lean we had to write less codes to  achieve the same  and we needed less engineers to do the  same work  so we were kind of convinced to go ahead  now our java engineers gladly were not  so negative about it started to  contribute to parallel work streams  and got taught to get some more  note skills and javascript skills  now the new stack at paypal changed over  and  what we have right now is c plus plus we  have  java serving some older apps and all the  new new apps  running nodes and i think the biggest  step into this direction for us was  actually decoupling our ui first  because obviously if your application  layer and the ui layer are  really tightly integrated it's very very  hard for you to  iterate and go ahead and start a new  stack  so now that we are able to run dust on  all those three different stacks  we can basically bring all new features  to all stacks which is amazing  for us now since we do javascript  templating  it comes with a big benefit that we can  run the render both  on the client side and the server side  and  we actually use that quite a lot so what  you will see if you go to paypal.com  the initial start page is always  rendered on server side and then for  dynamic content  we can decide on the fly if we want to  render that ahead or just when the  client really loads it up  so um one of the benefits of node is  obviously the node package manager  and we use it heavily to actually  distribute  our internal services so if we want to  use our monitoring services security  anything that actually interacts with  our c  plus tech we can just go ahead and  install some npm modules  and we're using an internal npm proxy  for that  which i will show in a few seconds as  well  we had this situation we were finally  able to uh  start and actually go ahead and use  nodes and we had  dust and everything was great right so  for us  this was the moment to release with  kraken  and the animation you just saw  took me about half an hour  so please bear with me so  with kraken we actually had this one  situation  where a lot of different teams have been  working on nodes they leveraged  express we used harpy lots of different  kind of  ways to build our servers and a few best  practices actually  started together so kraken is a  suit on top of express um it's not a  whole framework i wouldn't call it  framework  and it comes with a lot of kind of  sensible defaults a lot of  pre-configured stuff that makes sense  so as i said we used dust for templating  but it doesn't necessarily have to be  dust so if you like jade or if you like  ejs or anything else  you can just go ahead and use that and  originally it was made to make  middle scaled to corporate scales  companies  adopt node faster but we actually  noticed that lots of startups  started using nest and started using  kraken  because they didn't have to care about  security and a lot of other stuff  and it heavily taps into middleware  which obviously comes both  with express and cracking at moments  now the reason is as i said lots of  different teams at paper  are working on the same kind of stacks  and they build different apps  now if you have a lot of teams building  apps in the same frameworks or different  frameworks you will realize  everybody configures frameworks  differently  if you have now engineers moving from  one project into another  they're kind of lost because  configuration is made different  some of them might use some json files  for configuration  some of them use some methods to  initialize things so it was a big mess  and we tried to resolve that by bringing  some  best practices into that and now i  already said it before they have  actually more time to build projects  instead of just setting up the  environments which is really good for us  the project structure is actually kind  of opinionated  so we try to push for a clear model view  controller  way and we used that initially by having  um kind of different frameworks so we  used backbone and a lot of other  frameworks  but it was a bit too heavy for us so we  just went ahead and  did our own folder structure and that  works incredibly well for us  so we have those templates as i said can  be rendered both server and  client sides it comes with unit testing  straight ahead  and what is interesting about kraken is  that we moved the  logic away from our initial index.js or  app.js file  into the different controllers so  controllers handle routing  and a lot of other stuff and this is  something that you can actually see  is now also present in express four so  express three initially didn't have that  and they actually moved it over into  express four as well because they  realized it helps to  have much cleaner files and way more  structure  and i think the best for  modules and there is a whole lot of  different modules that come with it  amakara luska adaro and kappa  makara is used to do  internationalization localization or  specialization in your apps  lusca is coming with a lot of sensible  security defaults  adaro is used to bring dust.js support  and kappa is our npm proxy  now if you want to do a localization  it's actually quite easy we use key  value files for that  it's not json it's our own kind of  format with property files and you just  load  up lots of different content bundles and  you can do that  on the fly or you can obviously use  kraken to do that for you  but you don't have to use kraken to use  all of these modules  so what we do is we have a provider that  just loads for various locates  and it basically provides those  different values for you which is kind  of good  and the property files are not really  complex as i said it's not json syntax  but it allows for some structures where  you can bring arrays in there  you can define multiple values it's  actually kind of useful for us  you don't have to use this format but i  think it's actually pretty good since it  also supports some more structure like  sub keys  now if you want to use this it actually  heavily taps into  dust so one of the good things about it  is if you switch the local it  automatically  changes changes we locate for you in the  templates as well  by leveraging before folder structure so  different keys in different languages  get resolved for you  which is very handy and you can see that  down below with the header tag so it  basically just loads up the  index.creating  and index is my controller so  my localization is bound to my  controller and it just basically loads  in the file for me which is  really handy for us  and if i want to use adaro i basically  just defined dust as my view engine for  express  obviously i can still as i said before  use any other view engine  it's just dust for us because  a lot of view engines try to have a lot  of logic in their files and they  try to do a lot of processing for you  and calculations and that stuff and we  tend to move that  rather into our controller logic so  you basically have a layout and you  provide your powershells into it  and what happens is it fills up the  content so nothing new nothing magic in  there  dust comes with some cool stuff like  sections and you can provide your view  context into that  so different models can be just rendered  directly  and it also comes with conditioners  so not too much logic because it's just  saying  if a certain content is there show it or  don't show it  so it doesn't really try to be that  smart  now security is something that i think  is kind of interesting  lots of frameworks come with security  measurements and lots of kind of ways to  make your app secure and harden it but  lots of those frameworks also don't come  with sensible defaults so they just  assume you do the configuration  and quite often developers tend to not  do that  so one of the biggest features we  support is  prevention of cross cross site request  forgery for cserv  and it basically says if you want to  manipulate my data with  post requests put or delete you have to  provide a token  to make sure that it's really the app  and not somebody else click checking  which basically means  i'm framing your website in another  website  can be prevented by setting up the same  origin header  it comes with output escaping against  cross-site scripting and content  security policy support  and if you've attended last year's js  conf you might have seen mike west  talking about content security policy  it's really awesome stuff configuration  happens via  json so you basically just say i want  that feature i don't want to have that  feature  this is my setting or you can just go  through the method calls one by one so  you don't have to use all those kind of  things  now i already mentioned you have to  provide a token  and it's basically just a hidden input  field that you provide  over to the app and then it basically  validates it's usually your app  manipulating the data  the npm proxy kappa is very easy to  install you can go for that globally or  locally and that's basically what we use  to distribute our different services  like the keystore database access  logging monitoring and it's based on  another module called  npm delegate and if you look at the  configuration it's really just a folder  called config and in that folder you  have a bunch of different files  and you can set up various environments  so you can say this is my staging config  this is my development  this is my test environment and it's  pretty much  easy to set up in this case you see  the view engines and you can obviously  do that for all the kind of different  settings for kraken  now if you want to set up kraken with  let's say passport  you could go and really just configure  it by providing some options to it  and this is just the index.js file that  you would use for that  and now the nice thing is about that  you don't need to mess around with your  life cycle you don't need to  figure out when is my module being  loaded and why  you just tap into different events like  in this case middleware  so after my session is loaded i make  sure that my i can use my passport local  strategy  i just make sure that the user is nicely  serialized and deserialized and that's  it  so it provides a very very clean way to  make sure that my projects are nicely  and visible  so i think it helps a lot and  since we wanted to make sure that people  don't have to set up this stuff  manually and it comes with a yeoman  generator  so you do npm install generator kraken  call your cracking in your shell and  that's it  it's really easy to set up and i think  it is pretty cool because it  does a lot of scaffolding for you so it  comes with your templates it comes with  your localization with the tests  automatically  which is really useful for all of us i  guess  so kraken nowadays is already in version  1.x  which also came with the express four  push  and i think one of the features that we  will  focus on heavily is going to be  specialization which is not going to be  purely used for a b testing but also for  mobile device versus desktop and so on  so it allows us to do a lot of this kind  of logic  and well i can summarize for us it has  been actually  a really good right to introduce node  and especially kraken to the company  we have those really small teams with  lots of javascript full stack engineers  that are able to move much faster we can  write  our applications more efficiently we  have now dust as one  ui layer so we don't have to mess around  with different templates  and it was our very first big push into  open source  so for us it really helped to make sure  that we are not just building profit so  appropriate  sorry proprietary software for paypal  and release that to be wired  we actually tried to figure out what  would be useful for everybody  and our teams are now so convinced that  all the new apps are going to be  written on note purely big thanks for  attending my talk  and i'm open for some questions  you
 

